// Looper's own walking profile, replacing GraphHopper's bundled foot.json.
//
// The stock profile multiplies every edge's priority by `foot_priority`, an
// OSM-derived score built mostly from road class and sidewalk tags. For a
// loop generator aiming candidates in many directions, that meant every
// route funnelled onto the same "well-tagged" arterial roads regardless of
// bearing: to a walker one residential street is as good as another, but
// the stock weighting treated them very differently. This profile keeps the
// real constraints (impassable, dangerous terrain, private access) and
// drops the road-class preference, so distance and shape decide the route
// instead.
//
// The one road-class distinction that matters to Looper is a dedicated
// pedestrian way: where OSM maps a pavement separately, a pavement and its own
// carriageway weigh near enough the same that the router takes whichever is a
// few metres shorter, block by block, and the walk crosses and recrosses the
// road. That is confusing to follow and gives a spoken turn every time.
//
// The multiplier below is a tie-break, not a preference, and the difference is
// the whole lesson of `looper-foot-2`: it used 0.1, and because priority
// divides the weight, that does not say "prefer a pavement" — it says a
// pavement is worth walking ten times as far for. It bought every large detour
// to any footway nearby, legs came back 13-20% long, and at MAX_DISTANCE_ERROR
// of 0.12 those legs failed the distance gate and were asked for again. Two to
// three times the engine calls on urban ground, for a tie-break worth 20%.
//
// 0.8 is measured, not guessed — `bench/probe-pavement.mjs`, 2026-08-27, seven
// values over four town legs:
//
//   multiplier   douglas seafront   douglas inland   onchan   length
//   1.0                  3 hops            1 hop     3 hops        -
//   0.95                 5                 3         3        +0-2%
//   0.9                  3                 3         1        +2-3%
//   0.8                  1                 1         1        +2-3%
//   0.6 / 0.3            1                 1         1          +3%
//   0.1                  1                 1         5       +13-20%
//
// Two findings in that table. A *weak* nudge is worse than none: at 0.95 and
// 0.9 the route takes the pavement for some stretches and not others, which
// alternates more than leaving it alone. And 0.8 is the knee — 0.6 and 0.3 are
// identical to it, so 0.8 is the weakest push that gets the whole effect, and
// the one that buys the fewest detours. Douglas goes from 13% of the leg on
// pavement to 97%.
//
// Peel is the control and barely moves either way: it has fewest pavements
// mapped as their own ways. See `docs/routing-report.md`.
{
  "priority": [
    { "if": "!foot_access || hike_rating >= 2", "multiply_by": "0" },
    { "if": "country == DEU && road_class == BRIDLEWAY && foot_road_access != YES", "multiply_by": "0" },
    { "if": "mtb_rating > 3", "multiply_by": "0.7" },
    { "if": "foot_road_access == PRIVATE", "multiply_by": "0.1" },
    // A separately mapped pavement is normally a FOOTWAY. PATH, PEDESTRIAN and
    // STEPS are dedicated pedestrian links too, so they keep full priority. A
    // fifth of a road's weight is enough to settle the near-tie against the
    // carriageway beside it, and far too little to pay for a detour.
    { "if": "road_class != FOOTWAY && road_class != PATH && road_class != PEDESTRIAN && road_class != STEPS", "multiply_by": "0.8" }
  ],
  "speed": [
    { "if": "true", "limit_to": "foot_average_speed" }
  ]
}
